## Proof

Let $X$ be the unitary matrix such that $T := X^*YX$ and $S := X^*ZX$ are both upper triangular. Since $X$ is unitary, $X^* = X^{-1}$, so $Y$ and $Z$ are each unitarily similar to upper triangular matrices (i.e., $Y$ and $Z$ are simultaneously unitarily triangularizable).

Let the diagonal entries of $T$ be $\lambda_1, \lambda_2, \ldots, \lambda_n$ (the eigenvalues of $Y$) and the diagonal entries of $S$ be $\mu_1, \mu_2, \ldots, \mu_n$ (the eigenvalues of $Z$).

**Step 1: Reduce $YZ - ZY$ by the same unitary similarity.**

Since $X^*X = I$,
$$X^*(YZ - ZY)X = (X^*YX)(X^*ZX) - (X^*ZX)(X^*YX) = TS - ST.$$

Thus $YZ - ZY$ is similar (in fact unitarily similar) to $TS - ST$, and the two have identical eigenvalues.

**Step 2: Compute the diagonal of $TS - ST$.**

The product of two upper triangular matrices is upper triangular, and its diagonal entries are the products of the corresponding diagonal entries:
$$(TS)_{ii} = T_{ii}\,S_{ii} = \lambda_i\mu_i, \qquad (ST)_{ii} = S_{ii}\,T_{ii} = \mu_i\lambda_i = \lambda_i\mu_i.$$

Therefore
$$(TS - ST)_{ii} = \lambda_i\mu_i - \lambda_i\mu_i = 0 \quad \text{for every } i = 1, \ldots, n.$$

**Step 3: Conclude the eigenvalues.**

$TS - ST$ is upper triangular (being the difference of two upper triangular matrices) with every diagonal entry equal to $0$. The eigenvalues of an upper triangular matrix are exactly its diagonal entries, so every eigenvalue of $TS - ST$ is $0$.

Since $YZ - ZY$ is similar to $TS - ST$, it has the same eigenvalues.

**Conclusion.** All eigenvalues of $YZ - ZY$ are $0$. Equivalently, $YZ - ZY$ is nilpotent (with all $n$ eigenvalues equal to zero).

